{
 "id": "E54",
 "title": "Do brains still get better in a world of equals? Its later brains against its earlier ones",
 "thesis": {
  "claim": "In a world of equals brains no longer get better. The brains of a later iteration hold their own against those of an earlier one, and no more.",
  "why": "In Experiment 53's two runs with mutations of 10 numbers every agent does the same thing, a node's new brain is drawn by lot among its stakers, and from iteration 25,500 on the worlds hardly change. If every brain follows the same way of staking, none spreads better than another, and a contest between two moments of such a world is a tie. A score above a half would mean that brains still improve inside the order, which would make it more than an end point.",
  "confirm": "A coin is two contests with one deal, the two sides in swapped places; a score is the mean of its coins and its band two standard errors either side. Pooled over the two runs and both worlds of each pair, the later brains' share of the agents has a band that includes 0.5, for 25,000 against 23,000 and for 27,500 against 25,000.",
  "refute": "For either pair the whole band lies above 0.5: brains still get better in a world of equals. A band wholly below 0.5 for either pair is reported as undecided, as is anything else. Reported beside it: 27,500 against 23,000, and each run alone."
 },
